revision.text:
'''Kameido Tenjin Shrine''' is a Japanese [[Tenman-gu]] [[Japanese Shrine|shrine]] located in [[Kameido]], [[Kōtō|Koto Ward]] , [[Tokyo]]. The shrine is dedicated to [[Sugawara no Michizane]], a 9th-century Japanese scholar.<ref name=":0" /> == Description == The Kameido shine was built in honor of Sugawara no Muchizane, a prominent Japanese politician and scholar. Despite a long and successful career serving multiple Japanese emperors, Muchizane was eventually demoted from his position in the imperial court by the political machinations of [[Fujiwara no Tokihira]].<ref name=":1" /> Upon Muchizane's death several years later, a series of natural disasters struck Japan; some attributed these events to the ghost of Muchizane. To placate the departed scholar's spirit, a number of shrines were built (including one in Kyoto, then the imperial capital) in his honor, with most shrines portraying [[Tenjin (kami)|Muchizane as a kami]]; even after the disasters subsided, this tradition of honoring Muchizane's skill continued and many more shrines were built in his name.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://taiken.co/single/kameido-tenjin-shrine-an-impressive-shrine-worth-a-visit/|title=Kameido Tenjin Shrine - An Impressive Shrine Worth a Visit|work=Taiken Japan|access-date=2018-11-07|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>Morris, I. (1975). ''The Nobility of Failure: Tragic Heroes in the History of Japan''</ref>
The Kameido Tenjin Shrine was built in Tokyo in 1646. Tokyo was at time a port city of some importance, but was not yet the capital of Japan. Regardless, many people from Tokyo paid homage to Muchizane at the shrine between 1646 and 1945. The original Kameido shrine, built out of wood, was destroyed during [[World War II]] when the [[United States Air Force]] conducted a [[Tokyo firebombing|firebombing campaign against Tokyo]], resulting in the destruction of large parts of the city.<ref name=":0" /> In the years after the war, the shrine was rebuilt with more modern materials such as concrete.<ref name=":0" /> The restored shrine is famous for a number of its prominent features; the shrines contains a number of ponds bridged by [[Moon bridge|drum bridges]]. The shrine compound also has an abundance of plum trees.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1">{{Cite web|url=https://www.japan.travel/en/spot/1702/|title=Kameido Tenjin Shrine {{!}} Travel Japan - The Official Japan Guide|website=Japan National Tourism Organization (JNTO)|language=en|access-date=2018-11-07}}</ref>
Every year on 25 January, the custodians of the shire present a carved good luck charm to a visitor. Between April and May, the [[Fuji Matsuri]] festival is held at the shrine. The festival entails the celebration of flowers, namely the-then in bloom [[Wisteria]].<ref name=":0" /> == References == {{reflist}}

