ido, Tokyo (藤見物, 亀戸天神社 太鼓橋, 東京都江東区亀戸) (1914-09 by Elstner Hilton) clipped.jpg|thumb|On of the shrine's drum bridges in 1914]] The Kameido Tenjin Shrine was built in Tokyo in 1646. Tokyo was at time a port city of some importance, but was not yet the capital of Japan. Regardless, many people from Tokyo paid homage to Muchizane at the shrine between 1646 and 1945. The original Kameido shrine, built out of wood, was destroyed during [[World War II]] when the [[United States Air Force]] conducted a [[Tokyo firebombing|firebombing campaign against Tokyo]], resulting in the destruction of large parts of the city.<ref name=":0" /> In the years after the war, the shrine was rebuilt with more modern materials such as concrete.<ref name=":0" /> The restored shrine is famous for a number of its prominent features; the shrines contains a number of ponds bridged by [[Moon bridge|drum bridges]]. The shrine compound also has an abundance of plum trees.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":1">{{Cite web|url=https://www.japan.travel/en/spot/1702/|title=Kameido Tenjin Shrine {{!}} Travel Japan - The Official Japan Guide|website=Japan National Tourism Organization (JNTO)|language=en|access-date=2018-11-07}}</ref>
Every year on 25 January, the custodians of the shire present a carved good luck charm to a visitor. Between April and May, the [[Fuji Matsuri]] festival is held at the shrine. The festival entails the celebration of flowers, namely the-then in bloom [[Wisteria]].<ref name=":0" /> == References == {{reflist}}
